    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but i'm still having a hard time visualizing it. i get the pressure part with bernoulli, but how does newton's part actually happen at the same time? like, is the wing physically pushing the air down to get the reaction? i'm trying to see how the two things connect during actual flight.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand that visualizing these concepts can be challenging, but let me try to break it down for you. \n\nWhen an airplane wing moves through the air, it indeed both experiences pressure differences, as explained by Bernoulli's principle, and also interacts physically with the air, as noted in Newton's third law of motion. The wing's shape creates a pressure differential — lower pressure on top compared to the bottom side of the wing. This pressure difference can be thought of as the wing 'pulling' the air down, creating an upwards 'push' on the wing as a reaction (Newton's third law).\n\nAdditionally, the shape of the wing makes it more surface area on top compared to the bottom. As air collisions with the lower surface displace the wing downwards, the wing effectively pushes upward through the resistance. This opposing force, which includes both the pressure difference and the displacing air, results in lift.\n\nTo sum it up, the wing displaces air down and creates a pressure differential above and below it. Newton's third law then tells us there is an equal and opposite reaction to the downward force the plane experiences — which is lift, allowing the plane to rise against gravity as the wing’s shape and motion effectively 'p",
      "source": "target",
